            PPCCAAPP__TTSSTTAAMMPP__AADDAAPPTTEERR__UUNNSSYYNNCCEEDD - aaddaapptteerr__uunnssyynncceedd
                 Time  stamp provided by the network adapter on which the cap‐
                 ture is being done.  This is a high-precision time stamp;  it
                 is not synchronized with the host operating system's clock.

       Time stamps synchronized with the system clock can go backwards, as the
       system clock can go backwards. If a clock is not in sync with the  sys‐
       tem  clock,  that could be because the system clock isn't keeping accu‐
       rate time, because the other clock  isn't  keeping  accurate  time,  or
       both.

       Host-provided  time  stamps  generally  correspond to the time when the
       time-stamping code sees the packet; this could be some  unknown  amount
       of  time  after  the first or last bit of the packet is received by the
       network adapter, due to batching  of  interrupts  for  packet  arrival,
       queueing delays, etc..

       By  default, when performing a live capture or reading from a savefile,
       time stamps are supplied as seconds since  January  1,  1970,  00:00:00
       UTC,  and microseconds since that seconds value, even if higher-resolu‐
       tion time stamps are available from the capture device or in the  save‐
       file.   If, when reading a savefile, the time stamps in the file have a
       higher resolution than one microsecond, the additional digits of  reso‐
       lution are discarded.

       The  ppccaapp__sseett__ttssttaammpp__pprreecciissiioonn(3) routine can be used after a ppccaapp__ccrree‐‐
       aattee() call and after a ppccaapp__aaccttiivvaattee() call to specify  the  resolution
       of  the time stamps to get for the device.  If the hardware or software
       cannot supply a higher-resolution time stamp, the  ppccaapp__sseett__ttssttaammpp__pprree‐‐
       cciissiioonn()  call  will  fail,  and  the  time  stamps  supplied after the
       ppccaapp__aaccttiivvaattee() call will have microsecond resolution.

       When opening a savefile, the ppccaapp__ooppeenn__oofffflliinnee__wwiitthh__ttssttaammpp__pprreecciissiioonn(3)
       and ppccaapp__ffooppeenn__oofffflliinnee__wwiitthh__ttssttaammpp__pprreecciissiioonn(3) routines can be used to
       specify the resolution of time stamps to be read from the file; if  the
       time stamps in the file have a lower resolution, the fraction-of-a-sec‐
       ond portion of the time stamps will be scaled to the specified  resolu‐
       tion.

       The ppccaapp__ggeett__ttssttaammpp__pprreecciissiioonn(3) routine returns the resolution of time
       stamps that will be supplied; when capturing packets, this does not re‐
       flect  the  actual precision of the time stamp supplied by the hardware
       or operating system and, when reading a savefile, this does  not  indi‐
       cate the actual precision of time stamps in the file.

